# 3.3 EasyEDA Pro PCB return paths and grounding

Read this reference after reference copper exists and actual layout or routing must be checked for local return paths, layer-transition references, thermal grounding, or necessary ground vias.

The goal is to close real current and signal loops. Via count, regular grids, and edge fences are not goals by themselves.

```text
Read actual copper and routing
    → identify necessary returns
    → place and verify necessary vias
    → add optional shielding or global connection only when useful
    → repour, check, and save
```

## Confirm inputs

Confirm:

- current project and target PCB document;
- reference copper that was actually generated;
- current placement, tracks, layer transitions, and vias;
- target ground net;
- via geometry and manufacturing clearance;
- outline, holes, pads, tracks, and keepouts;
- power, sensitive-signal, ESD, thermal, or shielding regions to inspect.

If routing will still change substantially, finish the current critical paths before handling their returns. Do not generate speculative ground-via grids before signal paths exist.

## Use existing Actions

Relevant Actions include:

- `pcb-grounding-inspect`: read current pours, Regions, vias, and related components;
- `pcb-ground-vias`: plan, write, and verify bounded ground vias;
- `pcb-ground-stitching`: propose candidates for transition return, plane connection, or edge shielding.

Actual support comes from the current manifest, capability check, and execution result. A proposal does not prove a write or connection. Local geometry outside automation coverage may be completed in EasyEDA and verified from the actual result.

## 1. Inspect real return paths

Use current copper and routing to inspect:

- decoupling loops to the device power and ground pins;
- switching-power input, output, and device loops;
- exposed-pad, power-device, and thermal paths;
- ESD and interface-protection discharge paths;
- reference changes after a signal changes layer;
- continuous reference for high-speed, clock, analog, and other sensitive signals;
- detours caused by keepouts, slots, plane splits, and narrow copper;
- whether separate ground areas are actually connected.

Trace where current leaves, crosses the load, and returns. A shared `GND` name does not prove continuous copper, and a nearby via does not prove a useful return.

## 2. Place necessary ground vias

Prioritize vias that solve a clear issue:

- decoupling and local power loops;
- power parts and thermal pads;
- ESD and interface protection;
- reference ground near a signal layer transition;
- returns interrupted by layer or local geometry;
- explicit chassis or shield connections.

Each via group needs a clear object and purpose. Check:

- actual net;
- anchor and position;
- connectable copper on the target layers;
- clearance to pads, tracks, holes, edge, and keepouts;
- drill and annular geometry against manufacturing capability;
- effect on soldering, stencil, rework, and mechanical assembly.

Proximity is only a candidate condition. A via cannot close a return when no target copper exists, a keepout blocks it, or it lands in an isolated island.

Read back each new via by actual ID or exact geometry and confirm net, position, size, and count without changing existing vias.

## 3. Repour and verify

After adding or moving ground vias:

1. Repour affected copper.
2. Confirm each via actually connects to the target copper.
3. Check that the return path is more direct.
4. Inspect islands, necks, and unintended clearance.
5. Run configured DRC.
6. Visually inspect critical power, transition, ESD, and sensitive regions.
7. Save and read the result again.

A successful object creation does not prove that the return-path problem was solved.

## 4. Add global optimization only when useful

After necessary local returns, consider only when the design benefits:

- connection between ground planes;
- reference reinforcement in sparse regions;
- an RF, EMC, or shielding edge fence;
- additional returns around a specific interface or transition;
- large-area thermal or current distribution.

Global optimization needs a purpose and stop condition. Do not fill a regular grid or keep adding vias to reach a count.

A planner may find candidates, but accept them only after checking real copper, clearance, mechanical regions, and manufacturing capability. Write only candidates that improve the current return, shielding, thermal, or plane connection.

An area where no via fits safely may remain a known limitation. Do not force a candidate by violating keepouts or shrinking spacing.

## Failure and recovery

Follow [0.3 EasyEDA Pro Provider](../../0.3-easyeda-pro.md) and read current state again before recovery. Grounding recovery may complete or undo only vias and related copper objects that this operation can identify precisely. Do not reuse stale coordinates or geometry.

When one geometry cannot be resolved automatically, pause that region for a local manual check or actual readback. Manufacturing clearance, connectivity, keepout, and target copper must be resolved during PCB work. Only behavior that requires physical hardware becomes a prototype test.

## Finish this stage

Confirm:

- necessary local power and decoupling loops are closed;
- layer transitions have reasonable reference returns;
- ESD, thermal, and shielding connections match the design;
- new vias actually connect to target copper;
- pours are current;
- no obvious island, narrow channel, or unintended disconnect remains;
- DRC has no unexplained current issue;
- the PCB is saved.

Return to [3.1 PCB design and review](../../3.1-pcb-review.md) for the whole-board review. Enter [4.1 Manufacturing files and prototype ordering](../../4.1-production-handoff.md) only after the 3.1 exit conditions are met.
